HASTE — QUICKSTEP RUNE FOOTFALLS
Still source image for frame 1 of a dynamic 12-frame loop

STATUS

This brief produced the superseded HASTE quickstep effect, whose originals
remain archived under `assets_src/spells/haste/quickstep/`. It was replaced by
the user-approved orbiting-chevron animation. Do not treat this as the active
HASTE effect.

REFERENCE IMAGE INPUT

No reference image is attached. Do not use the HASTE loadout card as a
reference: it is an opaque full-scene illustration, not a battlefield effect
sprite. Create an original, isolated movement effect using the established
gold-and-turquoise HASTE palette.

PROMPT

Create exactly one still 2D fantasy-game VFX sprite: a vivid, freshly landed
magical quickstep, composed as the opening frame of a fast, alternating
footfall loop behind a moving allied unit. Render only the effect. The unit,
its feet, ground, terrain, projectiles, and battlefield must be absent; the
game will composite this transparent sprite around a live unit.

Make the motion read instantly at small battlefield scale. Near the lower
center, show one bright, elongated runic footfall mark made from a few bold,
broken turquoise arcs edged with warm gold light. Place a smaller, dimmer
echoing mark behind it, offset toward the left, so the two marks suggest a
rapid stride rather than a static emblem. Add a compact flash at the fresh
step and several crisp sparks and short angular streaks swept backward toward
the left. Keep the energy directional, asymmetric, and lively; favor a sharp
impact pulse and clear shapes over a soft, slow swirl or long motion blur.

Use abstract runic step marks, not literal shoe, boot, paw, or footprint
silhouettes, so the effect works for every unit type. Keep the active marks
low in the frame and mostly outside the central body area. The live unit will
be drawn over the effect, so leave the upper half and central torso area
mostly transparent. The source art should read as a fast movement accent,
not an attack, explosion, or damage effect. Its left-to-right motion vector
will be mirrored by the game for units facing the other direction.

ANIMATION INTENT FOR A LATER GENERATION

- Animate a quick left/right alternation of the two abstract footfall marks.
- Let each fresh mark flash brightly, then shrink and fade as it moves behind
  the unit.
- Send a few crisp sparks backward on each step; vary their positions so the
  loop has visible motion rather than repeating one still pose.
- Complete a seamless 12-frame loop with a brisk, even rhythm and no pause
  between steps. Keep the camera and gameplay anchor fixed.

TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS

- Output exactly one PNG at 1024 x 576 pixels, landscape 16:9, RGBA with
  genuine alpha transparency.
- Use a fully transparent background, not a checkerboard or a dark/colored
  matte. Only the runic footfalls, brief flash, and a few sparks may contain
  visible pixels.
- Keep all visible art inside the canvas with at least 64 px of transparent
  safety margin; do not clip sparks or glow.
- Use bold, clean silhouettes and crisp alpha edges that remain readable when
  scaled down. Avoid fine lettering-like marks and noisy particle clouds.
- No animation, sprite sheet, alternate takes, contact sheet, or variations.
- Save the source as `assets_src/spells/haste/quickstep-first-frame.png`.

NEGATIVE PROMPT

character, unit, person, face, body, feet, clothing, literal boot, shoe,
footprint, paw print, weapon, armor, fortress, catapult, projectile,
battlefield, ground, terrain, landscape, sky, scenery, opaque background,
checkerboard, flat background color, cropped sparks, clipped glow, dense
particles, large opaque glow, soft static aura, slow spiral, long smeared
motion blur, attack, impact explosion, fire, ice, lightning, damage, blood,
team emblem, faction symbol, text, letters, numbers, logo, UI, HUD, border,
frame, watermark, signature, photorealism, unrelated franchise, copied
commercial artwork

ACCEPTANCE CHECK

- The first frame clearly depicts a fresh, energetic step with one bright
  runic mark, one fading echo, and a few backward-swept sparks.
- The visual reads as speed at small size and remains distinct from an attack
  or damage effect.
- Abstract marks are suitable for all unit types; no character or literal
  foot is present.
- The upper and central body area stays mostly transparent, and the effect
  fits the fixed, mirrorable battlefield anchor.
- The background is genuinely transparent, with clean alpha edges and no
  matte, checkerboard, scenery, or opaque pixels outside the effect.
